# Domain Modeling in TypeScript

## Discriminated Unions (Coproducts)

Use a shared literal field (discriminant) to model states. This is the TypeScript equivalent of Scala's `sealed trait` + `case class`.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — boolean flags create impossible states
type Request = {
  loading: boolean;
  data?: string;
  error?: Error;
};

// ✅ GOOD — each state is a distinct shape
type Request =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: string }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error };
```

## Exhaustive Switching

Always handle every variant. Use a `never` check to get a compile error when new variants are added.

```typescript
function handle(req: Request): string {
  switch (req.status) {
    case "idle":
      return "Waiting";
    case "loading":
      return "Loading...";
    case "success":
      return req.data;
    case "error":
      return req.error.message;
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = req;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

## Nullability

- Use `T | undefined` for optional values. This is your `Option[T]`.
- Use `T | null` when the API or DOM convention uses `null`.
- With `strict` mode, the compiler enforces null checks — no need for a Maybe monad.
- Optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) are your `.getOrElse`.

## Product Types

- Prefer `type` for data shapes. Use `interface` when you need declaration merging (rare).
- Mark fields `readonly` by default.

```typescript
type User = {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly email: string;
};

const rename = (user: User, name: string): User => ({ ...user, name });
```
